<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derbyshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derbyshire is a county in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, including much of the Peak District. Its lead deposits were mined in Roman times, when lead pigs stamped with the name of the district were produced, and mining continued through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> period it lay in the kingdom of Mercia, and in the 18th century the Derwent valley mills made it a cradle of the factory system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derbyshire.jpg" alt="Derbyshire" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net). crop by A.D.Hope · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_Hathersage,_Peak_District_8_(cropped,_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
